==== Ethernet port ====
Some boards experience random problems with the ethernet port. This bug is likely caused by an misconfigured voltage on the ethernet pins at the SoC.

  * SoC: as default the RGMII pins are configured to operate at 3.3V
  * PHY: the Marvel 88E1518 can only operate at 1.8V

It turns out that the SoC can be configured via internal registers to set the correct voltage on the RGMII pins. The fix works ok with  bootloader from **1.34** firmware version (BUFFALO_BOOTVER=0.13). Most recent firmwares come with a different bootloader and probably using a different initialization for ethernet.

There are three ways to fix this bug:
  - **Setting the registers from userspace**: it can be done using devmem. But it requires building a custom firmware with ///dev/mem// enabled in the kernel, and the utility //devmem// in busybox: <code bash>devmem 0xd00184e0 32 0xa8a</code>
  - **Patching the bootloader**: Flashing a new bootloader might brick your device. This is the patched bootloader (BUFFALO_BOOTVER=0.13) from the firmware version 1.34. It's almost the same stock bootloader but with the fix made after the RAM initialization: <WRAP center round download 60%>
uBoot with the voltage fix: {{:media:buffalo:ls421de:u-boot.buffalo-1.34_voodoo.tar.gz}}
</WRAP>
  - **Patching the kernel**: this seems to be the best option. It won't require user intervention once Openwrt is installed. There is a patch already made for the kernel v5.4.41. But it wouldn't be accepted upstream. See: https://forum.openwrt.org/t/mvneta-help-with-a-voltage-patch/64226

Another workaround that can be made is to wire up the U29 IC (TPS3808) manual reset (pin 4) to a free GPIO. This way we can reset via software the ethernet chip everytime it fails:

{{ :media:buffalo:ls421de:ls421de-gpio_ethernet_reset.jpg?direct&300 }}

  * In the picture we soldered a very thin wire from the GPIO53 to the U29 reset pin. Let's initialize the GPIO53 as output in high state: <code bash>echo 53 > /sys/class/gpio/export
echo high > /sys/class/gpio/gpio53/direction</code>
  * When the ethernet hangs, we can perform a reset: <code bash>echo 0 > /sys/class/gpio/gpio53/value && sleep 1
echo 1 > /sys/class/gpio/gpio53/value</code>

=== Energy-Efficient Ethernet ===
The ethernet phy is [[wp>Energy-Efficient_Ethernet|EEE]] capable. Enabling this feature (using //ethtool//) may save some miliwatts. Example:
<code># ethtool --set-eee eth0 eee on</code>
Check if it has been enabled:
